n: Analyzing the essence of "class components" and "components"
2025-08-22
15
5-minute read
Column:=20
Autumn recruitment interview

Writing is architecture, and words are components.

In software engineering, a "component" is the smallest reusable and combina=
ble functional unit; and in knowledge expression, "deep writing" is also a =
componentization process of thought - encapsulating scattered knowledge poi=
nts into "cognitive components" with clear structure and self-consistent lo=
gic.

This article will use "deep writing" as a methodology to deeply analyze the=
 core concepts in front-end development: "Class Component" and "Function Co=
mponent", revealing their design philosophy, evolution path and engineering=
 value.

1. What is a =E2=80=9Ccomponent=E2=80=9D? From UI to cognitive abstraction
1. Component definition (general level)
Component : An independent unit that encapsulates specific functions, style=
s, and behaviors, with:

Encapsulation : internal implementation is hidden from the outside
Reusability : define once, use multiple times
Composability : multiple components can be assembled into a complex interfa=
ce
Testability : Independent units facilitate verification
For example: buttons, navigation bars, modal boxes, etc. are all UI compone=
nts.

2. Two implementation forms of components (taking React as an example)
type	English	Implementation	Features
Class Component	Class Component	Using ES6 classsyntax inheritanceReact.Comp=
onent	Support life cycle, state management, object-oriented style
Function Components	Function Component	Return JSX using a normal function o=
r an arrow function	Simple, functional programming style, used with Hooks
2. Class Components: Object-Oriented UI Encapsulation
1. Basic Structure (React Example)
class UserProfile extends React.Component {
  constructor(props) {
    super(props);
    this.state =3D { loading: true, user: null };
  }

  componentDidMount() {
    fetchUser(this.props.id).then(user =3D&gt; {
      this.setState({ loading: false, user });
    });
  }

  render() {
    const { loading, user } =3D this.state;
    if (loading) return &lt;div&gt;=E5=8A=A0=E8=BD=BD=E4=B8=AD...&lt;/div&g=
t;;
    return &lt;div&gt;=E7=94=A8=E6=88=B7=E5=90=8D=EF=BC=9A{user.name}&lt;/d=
iv&gt;;
  }
}
2. Core Features
characteristic	illustrate
Lifecycle methods	componentDidMount, componentDidUpdate, componentWillUnmou=
ntwait
this points to	There is thisa context, please pay attention to binding even=
t handling function
State Management	Use this.stateandthis.setState()
inheritance mechanism	Logic reuse can be achieved through inheritance (but =
not recommended)
3. Advantages and limitations
=E2=9C=85Advantages :=E2=80=8B

Suitable for complex components and centralized logic
The life cycle is clear and suitable for handling side effects (such as sub=
scriptions and timers)
Cons :=E2=80=8B

The code is lengthy and there are many template codes
thisError-prone (binding issues)
Difficult to optimize (closures are not as transparent as functional compon=
ents)
Difficulty reusing logic (HOC and Render Props are too complex)
3. Functional Components + Hooks: Functional UI Modeling
1. Basic Structure (Modern React)
import React, { useState, useEffect } from 'react';

function UserProfile({ id }) {
  const [loading, setLoading] =3D useState(true);
  const [user, setUser] =3D useState(null);

  useEffect(() =3D&gt; {
    fetchUser(id).then(userData =3D&gt; {
      setUser(userData);
      setLoading(false);
    });
  }, [id]);

  if (loading) return &lt;div&gt;=E5=8A=A0=E8=BD=BD=E4=B8=AD...&lt;/div&gt;=
;
  return &lt;div&gt;=E7=94=A8=E6=88=B7=E5=90=8D=EF=BC=9A{user.name}&lt;/div=
&gt;;
}
2. Core Change: Introduction of Hooks
Hook	effect
useState	State Management
useEffect	Side effect handling (alternative lifecycle)
useContext	Global state access
useReducer	Complex state logic
useCallback/useMemo	Performance optimization
=F0=9F=92=A1 The essence of Hooks : They allow function components to "reme=
mber" state and perform side effects, breaking the limitation that "only cl=
ass components can have state."

3. Advantages of Functional Components
=E2=9C=85 More concise : no need for class, constructor, this =E2=9C=85 Eas=
ier to read : logic is organized by function (useEffect separates side effe=
cts) =E2=9C=85 Easier to test : pure function + Hook, clear dependencies =
=E2=9C=85 Better reuse : custom Hook (such as useAuth, useLocalStorage) =E2=
=9C=85 More conducive to optimization : React team has more room for optimi=
zation of function components

4. =E2=80=9CDeep Writing=E2=80=9D is a Componentized Expression
Let=E2=80=99s change our perspective: think of =E2=80=9Cdeep writing=E2=80=
=9D as a =E2=80=9Ccognitive component=E2=80=9D .

1. Deep Writing =3D Content Component
property	analogy
title	Component Name
sections	Nested subcomponents
logic	Argument deduction (equivalent to business logic)
style	Presentation style (Markdown / HTML / LaTeX)
props	Input knowledge points and user needs
state	Thinking during the writing process (draft =E2=86=92 finished article=
)
hooks	Thinking tools: analogy, induction, deduction, visualization
2. Example: Encapsulating the "caching mechanism" as a knowledge component
&lt;ConceptComponent=20
  name=3D"=E6=B5=8F=E8=A7=88=E5=99=A8=E7=BC=93=E5=AD=98"
  type=3D"=E7=9F=A5=E8=AF=86=E7=BB=84=E4=BB=B6"
  inputs=3D{["URL=E8=A7=A3=E6=9E=90", "HTTP=E8=AF=B7=E6=B1=82", "Cache-Cont=
rol"]}
  output=3D{&lt;Article /&gt;}
  hooks=3D{[
    useAnalogize("=E7=BC=93=E5=AD=98=E5=83=8F=E5=9B=BE=E4=B9=A6=E9=A6=86=E5=
=80=9F=E4=B9=A6"),
    useStructure("=E5=BC=BA=E7=BC=93=E5=AD=98 =E2=86=92 =E5=8D=8F=E5=95=86=
=E7=BC=93=E5=AD=98"),
    useVisualize("=E6=B5=81=E7=A8=8B=E5=9B=BE")
  ]}
&gt;
  &lt;Section title=3D"=E5=BC=BA=E7=BC=93=E5=AD=98"&gt;...&lt;/Section&gt;
  &lt;Section title=3D"=E5=8D=8F=E5=95=86=E7=BC=93=E5=AD=98"&gt;...&lt;/Sec=
tion&gt;
&lt;/ConceptComponent&gt;
=F0=9F=A7=A0 This is exactly what you did in the beginning: decompose the c=
omplex process of "input URL to page display" into sub-modules such as "par=
se =E2=86=92 cache =E2=86=92 request =E2=86=92 render" in a componentized m=
anner .

5. Class Components vs. Function Components: A Paradigm Shift
Dimensions	Class Component	Function Components
Programming paradigm	Object-oriented (OOP)	Functional Programming (FP)
State Management	this.state	useState
side effect	Lifecycle Hooks	useEffect
Reuse mechanism	HOC / Render Props	Custom Hook
Maintainability	Medium (this trap)	High (logical aggregate)
Teamwork	Easy to understand but easy to write	Need to understand closures a=
nd dependency arrays
Future Trends	Has been gradually replaced	Mainstream recommendation method
=F0=9F=94=AEConclusion : Function components + Hooks are the standard parad=
igm for modern front - end development . Although class components are stil=
l available, they are no longer recommended for new projects.

6. In-depth writing suggestions: How to write technical articles using comp=
onent-based thinking?
Define the "article component" interface

interface ArticleProps {
  title: string;
  audience: 'beginner' | 'intermediate' | 'expert';
  concepts: string[];
  goal: 'explain' | 'compare' | 'teach';
}
Splitting "subcomponents"

&lt;Introduction /&gt;
&lt;ProblemStatement /&gt;
&lt;SolutionComponent /&gt;
&lt;CodeExample /&gt;
&lt;ComparisonTable /&gt;
&lt;Conclusion /&gt;
Enhance your expression with "Hooks"

useAnalogy(): Explain technical concepts with real-life examples
useTimeline(): Demonstrating technological evolution
useQnA()=EF=BC=9APre-set readers' questions and answer them
Support for "props expansion" allows readers to provide feedback through co=
mments and dynamically "update components".

Summary: Components are the universal language of the world
Whether it is a component in the front-end UI Buttonor a "deep writing comp=
onent" in knowledge expression, its essence is:

Encapsulation of complexity, abstraction of repetitive work, and pursuit of=
 systematization.

"Class components" represent the early object-oriented encapsulation concep=
t;
"Function components" embody the simplicity and combinatorial beauty of fun=
ctional programming;
"Deep writing" is the process of applying this componentized thinking to co=
gnitive construction and knowledge dissemination .
When you write an article, you are actually building a cognitive component =
that is spreadable, reusable, and iterative .

Conclusion=E2=80=8B=E2=80=8B

Programming is about writing components that machines can execute,
while writing is about writing components that the human brain can understa=
nd.
